---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle, APPS } from '../../content';

const path = '/alternatives';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'alternatives');
const desc = description(site, 'alternatives');
const alts = APPS.slice(1);

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Which alternative guide first?',
    answer: 'The app you are leaving — not the app you want. Each page names failure modes for that vendor\'s cart recovery approach.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Sequenzy always top replacement?',
    answer: 'No. Evaluate its published Shopify sync and email sequences against the events and purchase exits you need. Assign strategy, copy, QA, and reporting ownership separately from software selection.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Migration timeline?',
    answer: 'Plan cart and welcome first, then browse and winback after testing. Set the timetable from export completeness, event coverage, and a tested rollback rather than a fixed number of weeks.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Keep SMS on old platform during email move?',
    answer: 'Yes, if each channel and flow has one sending owner. Test purchase exits and opt-out propagation across both systems before allowing an email-to-SMS handoff.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Alternatives vs compare pages?',
    answer: 'Alternatives organize candidates by the problem you are leaving. Compare pages help define shared acceptance exercises for two finalists; neither page order nor a proposed exercise is an observed ranking.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Export before disconnect?',
    answer: 'Consent records, recovery timing docs, discount rules, integration map, 90-day recovery metrics.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Leaving Recart or SMS-only tool?',
    answer: 'Inventory the channels and flows you actually use. Rebuild only the required flows, and verify that consent and suppression records transfer before enabling a new channel.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Shopify Email after exit?',
    answer: 'Evaluate the native baseline first. Shopify Messaging now manages abandoned checkout automation. Stay if its event, recipient, and workflow coverage meets your requirements; add a provider for a demonstrated gap.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <h1 class="hero-title">Alternatives for cart recovery stacks</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-slate-700">
        Merchants switch cart recovery stacks when duplicate messages erode margin, SMS fees outrun attributed recovery, profile bills exceed email revenue, or recovery-only tools cap lifecycle growth. Start with the platform you are leaving, identify the missing job, and carry its event and suppression requirements into a replacement trial. The linked guides organize candidates by that job; their order is not a recovery-performance ranking.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox
          title="Migration hub rules"
          picks={[
            {
              label: 'Rebuild first',
              tool: 'Cart + welcome',
              reason: 'Highest revenue risk — test before cutover.',
            },
            {
              label: 'Overlap plan',
              tool: 'Test-defined',
              reason: 'Choose a window from event coverage and volume; keep one sending owner per channel and flow.',
            },
            {
              label: 'Schedule',
              tool: 'Peak readiness',
              reason: 'Avoid peak cutovers unless testing, staffing, and rollback capacity support them.',
            },
            {
              label: 'Measure',
              tool: 'Holdout 5%',
              reason: 'Illustrative allocation; choose sample size and duration before measuring incremental contribution.',
            },
          ]}
        />
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify card mt-12 max-w-4xl p-8">
        <h2>Why alternative guides exist for recovery stacks</h2>
        <p>
          Leaving a platform because of billing is a different decision from replacing a missing event, adding SMS, or buying production help. The Klaviyo guide separates connected email, multichannel setup, SMS, capture, and managed work. The AfterShip Email guide distinguishes marketing automation from shipment tracking. Use each guide's candidates and pricing questions to prepare a store-specific shortlist, rather than expecting a fixed number of ranked replacements.
        </p>

        <h2>Common exit triggers by platform type</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Event coverage:</strong> Identify cart, checkout, purchase, refund, and inventory data needed by each branch. <strong>Channel coordination:</strong> Decide whether email and SMS need a shared journey or an explicit handoff. <strong>Capture:</strong> Preserve signup source and offer context. <strong>Production capacity:</strong> Decide who writes, builds, approves, and maintains flows. <strong>Existing customer history:</strong> Keep identity and permission records usable across the move. These jobs may overlap; do not replace a whole stack to fix one missing field.
        </p>
        <h2>Historical names and the native baseline</h2>
        <p>Yotpo Email &amp; SMS and SMSBump remain linked for migration research, not a new sender shortlist. Yotpo announced full Email/SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025, and its current SMS page confirms decommissioning. Check retained exports and replacement ownership; this hub does not promise access to the retired service. See the <a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">migration announcement</a> and <a class="link" href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">decommission notice</a>.</p>
        <p>The historical Shopify Email entry should lead to a native-baseline decision: <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/migrate-abandoned-checkout">Shopify Messaging manages abandoned checkout automation</a>. Compare its actual installed coverage before assuming a second sender is necessary.
        </p>

        <h2>Migration checklist skeleton</h2>
        <p>
          Export consent with timestamps and source. Document live recovery timing, discount escalation, and suppression rules — screenshots are specs. Connect new platform to Shopify; verify cart events before import. Rebuild cart recovery first; test suppression with one active sender for each flow. Add welcome, browse, and post-purchase after cart checks pass. Confirm number portability and lead times with the SMS providers if changing service. Before cancellation, reconcile events, suppression records, and billing commitments; assess contribution with the agreed experiment and its uncertainty.
        </p>

        <h2>Split stacks after migration</h2>
        <p>
          A proposed split might assign capture to one tool, email to another, and SMS to a specialist. Write the handoff contract: signup source, eligible recipient, event ID, purchase exit, opt-out propagation, and offer priority. Inspect both queues after a purchase during an email-to-SMS delay. Keep a single sender per channel and flow; compare this maintenance effort with consolidation before choosing the split.
        </p>

        <h2>Pair with other hubs</h2>
        <p>
          Read <a class="link" href="/guides">attribution guide</a> before claiming migration win on dashboard revenue alone. Read <a class="link" href="/compare">comparisons</a> for finalist head-to-head. Read <a class="link" href="/apps">app directory</a> for holistic recovery fit beyond exit context.
        </p>

        <h2>Annual contract and renewal timing</h2>
        <p>
          Schedule an attribution audit ahead of the contractual notice deadline — if recovery margin flat and bill climbed, alternatives page for your vendor is the negotiation leverage. Check the current billing definition before assuming profile cleanup changes a Klaviyo or Omnisend invoice; minimums, commitments, and billable-status rules can change the result. If hygiene does not resolve the problem, schedule migration around the store's peak periods and support capacity. Document the test design and results for finance, including uncertainty and switching costs.
        </p>

        <h2>Data export checklist detail</h2>
        <p>
          Beyond contacts: segment boolean logic screenshots, flow delay and branch tables, discount codes used in recovery, SMS consent source per number, integration map (Shopify, Recharge, reviews app), 90-day recovery rate by channel, complaint and unsubscribe trends. Missing any item extends migration rebuild time — exports are migration specs not archives.
        </p>

        <h2>Parallel-send suppression keys</h2>
        <p>
          During the planned overlap, reconcile customer identifiers, last recovery send timestamps, and purchase events across systems. Test how guest checkouts and changed email addresses map. Log duplicates and pause the affected flow while investigating the cause. Record duplicate incidents and support complaints; do not infer a customer response threshold from the number of duplicate messages alone.
        </p>

        <h2>Rollback plan</h2>
        <p>
          Agree how long the old platform can remain available with flows paused, accounting for cost, retention settings, and contract terms. Set rollback criteria before cutover using event failures, duplicate sends, consent or suppression gaps, and contribution estimates with their uncertainty. Define rollback ownership and test whether the incumbent can resume without replaying already processed carts.
        </p>

        <h2>Hygiene versus migration decision tree</h2>
        <p>
          Run hygiene sprint when bill shock is primary pain but flows still convert: review engagement beyond opens alone, reconcile duplicate identities, and suppress contacts whose eligibility cannot be established, pause browse flows nobody maintains. Compare the resulting invoice and contribution before choosing renewal or migration. Unmaintained flows, documented duplicate sends, missing consent records, or lifecycle requirements beyond the current tool warrant investigation; a platform switch alone does not resolve these issues. For an active suite, price retaining only the required modules. Treat retired Yotpo Email/SMS as a replacement task rather than a module-renewal option. Document decision type in wiki: hygiene, prune, downgrade, or full migration.
        </p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-12 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
        {alts.map((a) => (
          <a class="card recovery-card" href={`/alternatives/${a.slug}-alternative`}>
            <h2 class="font-black">{a.name} alternatives</h2>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">{a.bestFor}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>

      <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Alternatives hub FAQ" />

      <article class="prose-shopify card mt-12 max-w-4xl p-8">
        <h2>Pricing-shape deep dive before any migration</h2>
        <p>
          Never migrate on a price screenshot. Record each quote's billable contacts, email allowance, SMS destinations and credits, minimum commitment, and required add-ons. Sequenzy's <a class="link" href="https://www.sequenzy.com/pricing">current pricing page</a> positions email pricing by sends with unlimited contacts; this can be useful when retained contact history is large relative to send volume. It does not establish the lowest total cost for your store. Model twelve months of audience growth, sends, channel fees, and operating work.
        </p>
        <p>
          Build separate audience and message forecasts: billable contacts by month, eligible abandonment events, expected email steps reached, SMS steps reached by destination, and other campaigns sharing the allowance. Do not multiply total profiles by a checkout-abandonment percentage: profiles and checkout events are different populations. Give finalists identical inputs and save dated quotes, including overages and service scope.
        </p>
        <p>
          Migration itself has a bill: export effort, rebuild hours, overlap with duplicate sends prevented, and rollback capacity maintained for an agreed period. Include the incumbent's actual notice period and commitments in the migration column, alongside software and labor costs. Finance should see the contribution comparison after discount depth, channel fees, and refunds before approving cutover.
        </p>
        <h2>Migration decision table</h2>
        <table><thead><tr><th>Your situation</th><th>Typical first move</th><th>Evidence and work to budget for</th></tr></thead><tbody>
          <tr><td>Bill shock, flows still converting</td><td>Hygiene sprint before any switch</td><td>Savings depend on billable-status rules and contract terms; compare the next invoice before deciding</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Flows lack an active owner</td><td>Leaving-app page plus staggered rebuild</td><td>Estimate the observation period from event volume; budget time for suppression and duplicate-send checks</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SMS-only stack, no email lifecycle</td><td>Graduation page for that vendor</td><td>Scope consent mapping, templates, event checks, and suppression tests before estimating the rebuild</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Suite sprawl, one active module</td><td>Module prune in place</td><td>Compare retained-module cost with replacement software and rebuild work</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Duplicate sends documented</td><td>Pause affected flow; trace ownership and suppression</td><td>Cutover decision must include support ticket trend, not margin alone</td></tr>
        </tbody></table>
        <p>
          Whatever row you land on, record the exit reason, inventory exports, demonstrate missing capabilities, and agree on observation and rollback before cutover. Use a suitably sized holdout where feasible; do not treat an underpowered result as a migration win. Pair the decision with the <a class="link" href="/guides">attribution guide</a> and the <a class="link" href="/guides">flow-collision guide</a> so the migration brief finance signs matches the flows that actually go live.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-shopify card mt-12 max-w-4xl p-8">
        <h2>Stakeholder alignment before migration</h2>
        <p>
          Retention, finance, support, and legal should sign migration brief: channel ownership doc, recipient eligibility and applicable messaging requirements, holdout plan, rollback criteria, peak blackout dates. Support needs FAQ for "why am I getting different emails" during overlap. Finance needs margin comparison methodology not dashboard screenshots. The responsible reviewer checks SMS permission evidence, scope, and any gaps; export completeness alone does not establish legal compliance. Missing decisions can delay cutover; assign an owner and acceptance criteria to each unresolved item.
        </p>
        <p>Migration war room: daily 15-minute duplicate-send check during overlap. One owner with authority to pause flows if collision detected.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-shopify card mt-12 max-w-4xl p-8">
        <h2>Connected email versus managed execution</h2>
        <p>Sequenzy's <a class="link" href="https://www.sequenzy.com/features/shopify-integration">Shopify integration overview</a> describes customer and order sync, purchase-based segments, and cart, post-purchase, and winback email sequences. Those capabilities make it a candidate for connected lifecycle email. Demonstrate the required branch with your own event payload and a purchase while a reminder is queued. Published capabilities do not establish superior strategy or migration fidelity.</p>
        <p>If the missing job is production capacity, attach a service scope to the shortlist: who briefs the flow, writes copy, approves discounts, checks rendering, monitors failures, and exports the finished work on exit? Compare the agreed deliverables and ongoing owner effort. A software subscription and a managed production agreement solve different parts of the problem.</p>
        <h2>Acceptance record for a proposed migration</h2>
        <p>Save the trigger payload, customer identity, expected branch, actual queue state, and result for each rehearsal. Cover guest checkout, changed email address, repeat checkout, purchase during a delay, opt-out, and unavailable stock. For AfterShip Email, identify the marketing automation rather than assuming a shipment notification is the recovery trigger. Use enough representative delayed-order events to assess the required exits; a calendar deadline alone cannot supply missing coverage.</p>
        <p>Compare contribution per eligible customer over a common outcome window, including orders without a tracked click. Deduplicate orders across senders and subtract discounts, goods, fulfillment, payment, message, and return costs. If traffic supports a randomized holdout, agree allocation and duration before observing results. Report uncertainty alongside the estimate; an attribution increase alone does not establish incremental lift.</p>
        <h2>Choose the smallest justified change</h2>
        <p>For bill pressure, check billable-status rules and the next invoice after eligible cleanup; do not promise a fixed savings percentage. For a missing branch, demonstrate the exact event and exit before replacing other flows. For duplicate sends, pause the affected flow and resolve shared ownership first. For a retired sender, reconcile retained permission and suppression history before enabling a replacement. For native Shopify recovery, keep it when installed coverage satisfies the job instead of migrating at an arbitrary order threshold.</p>
        <p>Keep a dated decision record with the exit reason, candidate evidence, quote assumptions, unresolved gaps, named approvers, cutover owner, and rollback procedure. Revisit it before renewal or a material catalog/channel change. The <a class="link" href="/compare">comparison hub</a> supplies common finalist exercises; the <a class="link" href="/apps">app directory</a> distinguishes current evaluation questions from historical migration entries.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
